 {:seon.task/name "the-agent-is-the-unit-not-the-run"
  :seon.task/plan "A2 as built next (the owner, 2026-10-30: \"I want the messages as facts on main also\"): a message on main is addressed to the agent by its branch, :seon.message/branch (the agent's identity across boots and wakes, what A4 makes its key), never to a run: its two writers on main, seon.root's :message (today (get-in st [:view :agents id :run])) and seon.reconcile's verdict message (to the run), write the branch; its readers by branch drop the run join: seon.agent/untaken-entries, seon.reconcile branch-messages, observe's message count, taken-messages and last-verdict; seon.task/message-tx takes the branch and loses the run placeholder; a parked agent wakes when main holds more messages to its branch than it took. Owners, 2026-10-30: A2 and A4 the context session's, B (the verdict a merge check on the task by commit) offered to the optimization session. The merge design needs no change: it is keyed by branch and commit (the merge base, main's commit), its catch-up fact by the agent; a turn merges main whenever main moved (9e93b0afcd).\n\nThe owner, 2026-10-29: \"get rid of runs, the concept doesn't really exist and is just X amount of forms being evaluated ... An agent is just writing evals ... an attribute for the turn limit and extend the turn limit to extend a run ... by default show everything: get rid of the limits for evals and turns; messages at the newest 100 is fine\"; then \"Do it\".

What exists: a run (:seon.run/run-fact, ~280 src and ~200 test references; seon.reconcile 138, seon.task 72, seon.problems 40) is the reconciler's unit of work. It holds four kinds of data: lifecycle (status, outcome, reason, start-ms, end-ms, error, taken, holder, boot), the judge's verdict (results, deferred, deferred-results, conflicts, unmerged, changed, uncontracted, caused, behind, gate-unread, failures, costs, task-conflicts, commit, commit-error, merged-t, basis-t, plan), identity (id, task, agent, branch, env, model) and the turn budget (max-turns, turns, tokens, model-calls, waste). Turns (:seon.turn/run) and messages (:seon.message/to) point at a run; the agent's pull pattern (:seon.db/agent-fact's :seon.ctx/pattern) reaches its task through [:seon.run/_agent :as :runs], windowed by :seon.config.ctx/window (100) on runs, evals, turns and catch-ups. seon.render.ui/render-ctx (then also chat-ctx, since deleted) found the task through the-run and held-task.

Where each part goes:
- lifecycle on the agent (:seon.agent/status exists; its last end as :seon.agent/outcome and :seon.agent/reason; start and end are its first and last turn);
- the verdict on the task, as facts keyed by the commit it judged (with seon.proofs/test-results-are-a-few-facts);
- identity deleted (the agent holds its key, branch and task: a new ref :seon.agent/task);
- the budget as :seon.agent/max-turns, raised to extend the agent; turns, tokens and model calls derived from its turns; waste derived or deleted.

Cut 1, the agent is the unit for the context and the page (the reconciler unchanged):
1. Schema (resources/seon/schemas/seon.facts.edn): :seon.agent/task (ref to the task), :seon.agent/max-turns, :seon.turn/agent (ref to the agent); messages joined to the agent (:seon.message/agent, written beside :seon.message/to by seon.task/message-tx's taker, the turn that takes it).
2. The agent's pattern: [* {:seon.agent/task :seon.task/task-fact} {[:seon.turn/_agent :as :turns] [*]} {[:seon.eval/_agent :as :evals] [*]} {[:seon.message/_agent :as :messages :limit :seon.config.ctx/window :reverse true] [*]}]: no window on turns and evals; runs and catch-ups out of the pull (catch-ups stay facts on the branch; their one reader is the pull).
3. seon.agent/begin-run! writes :seon.agent/task; seon.turn writes :seon.turn/agent on every turn (beside :seon.turn/run, which the reconciler still reads), and work! reads its budget from :seon.agent/max-turns when set.
4. seon.render.ui: the-run and held-task replaced by (:task c) and (:turns c) in render-ctx, conversation, status and run-head.
5. seon.context/records and turn-pages query by the agent (:seon.turn/agent, :seon.message/agent), not through :seon.run/agent.

Cut 2, the reconciler schedules agents: seon.reconcile/actions, advance and classify read :seon.agent/status and the task; judge-tx and verdict-body write the verdict on the task by commit; seon.task/run-start-tx, run-value, runs and end-members become the agent's spawn facts; seon.problems/run-rows becomes agent and task rows; my.root and seon.server list agents; a message is to a task's agent (or the task, before one exists); turn ids become <agent-key>/<n>, n counting on across wakes. The owner's choices (2026-10-29): a message is addressed to the agent, whose entity root writes on main when it decides to work a task, before the process forks; the verdict is on the task, one fact per branch commit judged, read one hop out from the agent; an agent at its turn limit, extended (:seon.agent/max-turns raised) or woken by a message, goes on as the same agent entity, branch and conversation, its turns counting on (a dead process's successor takes up the same entity). Cut 2's further choices (2026-10-29): an agent is one per branch, keyed agent/<branch>, the same across boots and wakes (today's session: a work task and its merge share a branch), :seon.agent/task the task it is on now, its process (id, pid) attributes that change per spawn; :lost and :killed are agent statuses, :seon.agent/reason saying why, a lost agent respawned as the same agent; one agent works a task at a time; root's judge and verdict take git's words (the owner: \"more git merging terms ... less made up things\"): a merge check of a commit, its result :seon.merge-check/* on the task (task, commit, base, main-t, mergeable, reasons, tests, behind, conflicts), accept is merge. Steps: A, the agent's key by branch, root writing the agent before the fork, messages to the agent, turn ids <agent-key>/<n>; B, the merge-check result entity, read by done-input, seon.problems, my.root and the server; C, the lifecycle on the agent (seon.reconcile/classify, idle, parked, the status cas, flights by agent, reap and kill on the agent).

Cut 3: :seon.run/* and :seon.config.ctx/window on turns and evals deleted from the schemas, docs/ctx.md, docs/render.md and the help. The standing store is erased when cut 3 merges (the owner, 2026-10-29: erase when cut 3 merges, asked once more then; AGENTS 9).

Replaces: the run entity, its pattern join and the window on an agent's own record. Deletes, at cut 3: :seon.run/run-fact and its ~280 src references.

The owner, 2026-10-30 (docs/decisions.md#there-are-no-runs-an-agent-has-an-env-a-branch-and-its-evals): runs go entirely. The steps, from the inventory of that day:
# Runs out: the plan by steps (task seon.agent/the-agent-is-the-unit-not-the-run, cut 2 and 3)

Inventory (Haiku, 2026-10-30, src/): 501 occurrences of :seon.run/* on 348 lines; by kind lifecycle 139,
verdict 121, identity 199, budget 19, other 23. reconcile 198, task 97, problems 67, server 37, agent 26,
ui 25, turn 15, my.root 14, context 4, proc 4. :seon.turn/run 6, :seon.message/to 9.
Owner, 2026-10-30: \"there is no concept of a run ... Agents have envs and run evals and take steps and we
have max steps that we can increase if they hit their limit\"; at the limit an agent parks with its process
and context, and raising :seon.agent/max-turns (or a message) wakes the same agent.

A1. Readers find the agent's task through the agent, not its newest run (no schema change):
    seon.context/status (context.clj ~918), my.agent/problem, seon.render.ui/own-problems, newest-run,
    run-head, agent-card, debug-input, seon.render.proc/agent-view and main-info, seon.server/run-of and
    live-run, seon.task/session-key and this-boots, seon.problems/own?: read :seon.agent/task,
    :seon.turn/agent, :seon.agent/max-turns. seon.problems/own takes the agent, not a run entity.
A2. Messages to the agent: seon.task/message-tx writes :seon.message/agent (no run placeholder);
    readers by branch and :seon.message/to (reconcile branch-messages, taken-messages, last-verdict;
    seon.agent/untaken-entries) read by the agent.
A3. Turns: ids <agent-key>/<n>, n counting on across wakes; seon.turn/act! and context-block write
    :seon.turn/agent only.
A4. The agent keyed by its branch (agent/<branch>), written by root on main before the fork.
B.  The verdict a merge-check result on the task, keyed by the commit judged (:seon.merge-check/*: task,
    commit, base, main-t, mergeable, reasons, tests, behind, conflicts): seon.reconcile/judge-tx,
    advance's gate and merge writes, seon.task/end-members' verdict half, my.root/gate!; read by
    done-input, seon.problems, my.root, seon.server.
C.  Lifecycle on the agent (:seon.agent/status, outcome, reason; start and end its first and last turn):
    seon.reconcile classify, idle, parked, the status cas, flights by agent, reap and kill;
    seon.task/run-start-tx and status-tx become the agent's.
3.  Delete :seon.run/* (schemas, seon.task's run fns, the run patterns, docs/ctx.md, render.md, help);
    erase the standing store when it merges (ask the owner once more).
Each step its own branch, its readers' tests named, handed to the optimization session to prove and merge.
"
