 {:seon.task/name "a-taken-test-run-is-not-the-forms-work"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30 (md-scan #26): an agent's eval (my.agent/prove) took root's :tests in place (seon.agent/await-op!, *waiting*: the run read inline on the eval's thread) and the eval's byte budget (:seon.config.agent/form-bytes, 1 GiB) stopped it at 1,076,290,608 bytes after 1 s. The test run is the test service's work done in this process, not the agent's form: it should run outside the eval's tick and byte budgets, as root's platform work and work!'s loop do (seon.repl/budgeted); its tests carry their own per-test budget (:seon.config.agent/test-ticks). A nested seon.intr/run does not suspend the outer budget (measured: a run with no budget inside one of 10 MB still spends the outer's), and the budgets count per thread. First measure where a prove's 1 GiB in the agent process goes (test-plan, reading the child, record-results!, the finish): it may be waste. Then either the in-place run goes to the handoff thread the idle loop uses (*waiting* false: the children on an io-thread, ::say and ::ran back through the intake, which await-op! already takes), with what runs on the eval's thread made cheap, or Jolt gives a way to pause a budget. Until then an eval's (my.agent/prove) can come back :seon.repl/bytes-spent, which the agent reads as its own form's fault. Measured 2026-10-30 in a test root's agent (one changed test): the first seon.agent/test-plan allocates 161 MB (seon.program/reach-indexed 120 MB, the reach index built), a prove after it 51 MB on the eval's thread (36.7 MB in seon.sock/recv!, the child's reports read inline; test-plan inside run-tests-forked 32 MB), the next 16 MB (branch-changes 8.9 MB, program-digest 6.6 MB). Tried and dropped: running the in-place :tests by the handoff path (*waiting* false) leaves the plan and prove!'s own digest on the eval's thread; a prove under a 30 MB budget stopped at 32.9 MB either way. So the exemption is the whole in-place handling: a Jolt way to pause a run-interruptible budget around it (the cleanest: the budget counts the form's own work), or the handling on a thread of its own with the agent's writes still made by the loop."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-budget-comes-from-measured-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: a budget where 90%+ of the time it's under budget and the rare overrun is mostly a time we should intervene (docs/decisions.md). What exists: an eval's tick and byte budgets are fixed config (:seon.config.agent/form-ticks 20 G, :seon.config.agent/form-bytes 1 GiB), applied by seon.repl to the origins seon.repl/budgeted names (:model, :outside, :ui), counted by seon.intr/run; a test's tick budget is derived from its own history (seon.fx/test-budgets: min(cap, max(floor, factor x the median of its green ticks))). The fixed byte budget stopped root's whole-program loads (fixed: root's evals unbudgeted), an eval's (my.agent/prove) (task seon.agent/a-taken-test-run-is-not-the-forms-work) and seon.agent-test/a-test-childs-ceiling-is-its-own. Plan: (1) measure the population first: every recorded agent eval's :ticks and :bytes by origin, from the md-scan bench store's run branches (main holds only root's own evals: 32 records, 2026-10-30), p50, p90, p99, max; (2) one budget function for tests and evals, budget-of [samples {:quantile :factor :floor :cap}] (the per-test budget is the case quantile 0.5 over one test's greens), seon.fx/test-budgets calling it; (3) an eval's budget per origin is budget-of over the recorded evals of that origin (quantile and factor so that 90%+ of past evals pass, config keys with their provenance), derived where an agent is forked (root, from the population at t; derived data, regenerated when evicted), the fixed keys becoming the cap; (4) an overrun stays the typed error to the agent (:seon.repl/ticks-spent, :seon.repl/bytes-spent) and is recorded as a fact, an intervention point root can show. Open: the budget's unit of scope (per eval now; per fiber once the agent's intake dispatcher runs a request on its own fiber: the context session's measurement of whether Jolt fibers share the per-thread counter). Proofs to write: budget-of's cost and quantile behavior; an eval within the derived budget runs; one past it is the typed error; the test budgets unchanged (same numbers before and after)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agent-is-a-flow-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "Step (1) of THE PLAN's agreed order (docs/plan.md#where-it-stands-2026-10-30-morning): remove the hang class. The owner, 2026-10-30: move more things to Flow, no hand-written ways of controlling processes, Clojure's and its libraries' words. What exists: the agent reads root's socket by hand (seon.agent/intake, a fiber; recv-raw!, poll!, next-msg!), and several waits take from it (the idle loop under seon.step/run-loop, seon.agent/await-op! inside prove!, fx! and clone!, seon.agent/inbox), each putting what it does not want on a shared :pending queue; a :tests request is handled by whichever wait takes it (*waiting*, take-op, answered-anyway!, and the ::say/::ran handoff in seon.agent/handle!). md-scan #21 hung on a :tests left in :pending, #26 on one abandoned when its eval's byte budget stopped it. Root's side is already a Flow proc on a jolt.async.remote end (seon.agent-proc/agent-proc). Measured in scratch Jolt processes, 2026-10-30: a Flow graph of :io procs on the main carrier stays one active thread through start, inject, ping and stop, and forks at every point; a fiber's allocation is not charged to a parked eval's byte budget (the same allocation in the eval stops it at 10.88 MB of 10 MB); an eval blocked in one proc's step, waiting on a promise, gets its answer while another proc serves root's nested request; an interrupt stops an eval parked on a promise or a channel (206 ms after its token was set); ping leaves a proc that is inside a step out and the graph does not wedge. Research: one reader routing replies by id and requests apart (nREPL's :id, LSP's every request answered even when cancelled, Erlang's monitor answering a dead callee's caller); Flow has no request/reply primitive, a request carries its reply-to [pid in-port] (the Flow guide). The change, in steps, each its own commit with a test watched failing first: (1) the agent's frames come to it on one channel and leave on one: today's reader fiber (seon.agent/intake, seon.sock/recv!, which takes a descriptor sent with a frame: the clone's socket, seon.agent/clone!) is the :transport proc's in-port, and every proc sends its frames whole itself (a write blocks the thread, so no two frames interleave); a jolt.async.remote end (as root's is) once Jolt's end carries a descriptor with a value (task jolt.async/a-remote-end-carries-descriptors; its reader recv(2) drops SCM_RIGHTS today), which deletes seon.sock's agent side; serve! runs a Flow graph of :io procs, :transport (the end's :in is its in-port; routes requests by :op, answers by :id) and :repl (agent-step's evals, :judge, :merge-main, :render, one at a time), in place of seon.step/run-loop; root's :pause, :resume, :stop and :ping frames map onto Flow's own; the lib/macos? gate in seon.repl/run! goes (one loop on every platform; the Linux gaps are Jolt's: jolt.io-poller/wake-on-signals-on-linux, jolt.async/a-forked-child-closes-inherited-ends-on-linux); measured: an agent's ready time and memory against main's (ten agents ready in 172 ms). (2) a :tests proc serves root's :tests on its own fiber (plan, fork the child, read its pipe, answer); prove! injects its request with a promise and derefs it; deletes *waiting*, take-op, answered-anyway!, ::say and ::ran. (3) every wait (fx!, the clone's :fd and :branched, inbox) is a promise the transport holds by :id; the agent's own requests carry :id, not :k; at the end's close every promise held is delivered :dead; deletes :pending, next-msg!, poll!, recv-raw! and await-op!'s loop. (4) a step that throws reaches Flow's error channel and its message is answered by its :id with the error (seon.root/answer-taken's pattern, reused). (5) seon.turn/work! checks :seon.agent/max-turns once, after the green check and before any model call; a turn that threw goes round. (6) measured against main: src lines, a prove's ticks and bytes, ready time and memory; then md-scan run 28 on a main of its own (SEON_MAIN=bench-28). Wiki: docs/processes.md (the agent process, the socket and the agent proc), docs/flow.md (Flow inside an agent), docs/failure-points.md, docs/turn.md, docs/clocks.md (Flow's stop and ping bounds in agents). Adapted as it is learned: steps (1)-(3) are one change (with only a transport, the waits inside an eval would still read the same channel); step (4), the owner's ruling of 2026-10-30: a step that throws answers its request with the error and the agent goes on (seon.repl/defect-answer), where it ended the process with exit 70 and the run :error (seon.agent/died!, deleted). Measured 2026-10-30 on jolt/agent-flow: src +185 -239 (net -54); a three-proc graph starts in 0.06-0.17 ms; a prove of a changed branch while an eval blocks answers in 64 ms (main: it waits behind the eval for good); (my.agent/prove) under a 30 MB byte budget passes while the run allocates 60 MB on the thread."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-tests-request-is-answered-while-prove-waits seon.agent-test/a-tests-run-is-not-charged-to-the-eval-that-waits seon.agent-test/a-stopped-evals-tests-request-is-still-answered seon.agent-test/root-gone-ends-every-wait seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on seon.agent-test/every-turn-throwing-ends-at-the-cap}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-limit-fails-loudly"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: no artificially low limits, and every limit fails loudly; a budget is useful to catch problems if it is used right. The limits audit (read-only, 2026-10-30) of resources/seon/config/default.edn and src/ found these silent or weakly announced limits on what an agent sees, first to fix: (1) :seon.config.ctx/errors 5: an agent's context shows only its five newest errors, older ones unannounced (against the ruling: no cuts on an agent's own errors); (2) :seon.config.ctx/window 100: the oldest evals, turns and messages of an agent's record drop out, from one sample (63 evals over 4 runs), reachable at 40 turns; (3) an agent's own contract errors, test failures and stderr cut at :seon.config.render/part-budget 256 tokens with no handle to read the rest (seon.contract, seon.repl/serve!, seon.server); (4) event channels with sliding buffers that drop silently on overflow (seon.root's 8192 and 1024, seon.render.proc's 4096 and its 2048 a pass): a waiter on a dropped event hangs. Then the guessed or tiny-sample limits that are loud: :seon.config.agent/form-ticks 2e10 (from tick rates, not evals: check it on md-scan evals as form-bytes was), :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes 1 GiB (a round number, the same class as form-bytes), :seon.config.ai/max-tokens 8192 (is a cut reply told to the agent?), :seon.config.agent/test-ticks 1e10 (1.2x the costliest green), the merge limits (max-conflicts 3, max-merge-turns 2, max-behind 3: guesses), ai/max-in-flight 8 (stale beside max-running 10), the shutdown bounds of batch 32. Correct: each limit either removed, or derived from measurements with its provenance, and each reached limit a typed error or a recorded problem naming itself; a cut of an agent's own value is never unannounced and always carries the form that reads the rest. Ask the owner whether announced cuts with a handle (the 16000-token value budget) count as cuts. The owner, 2026-10-30, on items (1) and (3): errors render grouped by signature with their counts, the newest first line and a handle; root records all; the context sends what changed (\"isn't that a diff like we are already doing?\"; \"the value printer needs to be smarter to not just print everything and make it clear that there are a shitload of errors\"). Plan for (1) and (3), the context session's: an agent's errors reach its context once, from one producer, seon.problems/own (the page's own-problems part: every error last raised in its task, one row per signature, :seon.error/count, root's records), whose report is sent the model as grouped lines, not printed whole: seon.render.ui/problems-model, the declared :seon.render/model of :seon.problems/report, each class with its count and its rows newest first, a row its count, type, the message's first line and the form that reads it whole, (my.agent/problem signature) (the row, its example and evidence: new, a few lines over seon.problems/own). Deleted: the task record's :errors join (seon.facts's :seon.task/task-fact pattern, its :limit :seon.config.ctx/errors 5: a second producer of the agent's errors that nothing in src reads), the :seon.config.ctx/errors key and its schema, and its cost proof seon.ctx-test/a-pull-costs-what-the-task-cites-not-the-stores-history. Item (2), the window on an agent's own messages and turns, follows as its own change."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-problems-report-is-sent-grouped-with-its-handles}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-child-that-dies-says-why"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30 in four full proves (batch 48's, ratom-teach's twice, and one more): bin/check scale fails because 2 to 4 of its 100 agents answer their test run {:pass 1 :fail 0 :error 1 :test 1 :reports []}: an error with no report, the child's exit 0, its heap ~337 MB (the :heap of f-test 336-337 MB every time); green in three other full proves the same day. A test child that errors and reports nothing breaks the principle that every failure comes back as data naming its cause (AGENTS section 4, nothing crashes and nothing hangs). Correct: find what the error is (the child's stderr, its exit and signal, its heap against :seon.config.agent/trip-bytes and the inherited bytes; the 337 MB heap of a one-assertion test is itself the first suspect), make the child's death or refusal a report with its cause, then fix the cause. Evidence: re-run bin/check scale under load (another prove beside it reproduced it), keep its scale.log. Found since (batch 49 made the report reach its asker, seon.proofs/answer): the error is :seon.test/pipe-held, \"the test process exited and a process it forked still holds its pipe (its end of file never came)\" (batch 51's prove, 1 of 100 agents). So a process other than the test child holds the pipe's write end: one the agent forked at the same moment (a sibling fork, or a spawned command without close-on-exec) inherits it. Next: name the holder in the report (the pipe's other holders, from the fd's inode, when it is held), then close the write end in every fork but the child's (the fds atom run-all keeps covers only test children, and only once each is registered)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-test-child-that-dies-reports-why}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-first-request-allocates-the-same-in-any-shard"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.agent-test/an-agent-allocates-little-from-ready-to-its-first-request is flaky: in batch 61's merge prove (2026-10-31, at 8cfb992de2) it measured 18,212,464 bytes from the agent's :state mark to its first request against its 12 MB bound, and green alone on the same tree and on main (alone it is ~6.5 MB, measured 2026-10-29). So what an agent allocates before its first request depends on what ran earlier in its shard, which shares one test root and fork server (seon.test-store/agent-tests-in-one-process-share-its-root). Likely cause, to measure, not assumed: the fork server primes the last schema hash (seon.db/prime-commit!) so its agents' first commit hashes no schema; an earlier test that registered a schema or changed main's leaves that key stale, and the agent hashes Seon's whole schema again (~12 MB; 18.2 - 6.5 = 11.7). Reproduced 2026-10-31 without shard order: a plain `bin/test seon.agent-test` (68 tests, one shared root) measured 12,261,984 bytes at agent a67, the namespace's 67th agent, against ~6.5 MB alone; in batch proves 18.2, 13.2 and 12.05 MB. So an agent's start-up allocation grows with the root's history (main records every earlier test's runs, agents and messages, and each agent's branch is cut from that main), not with which test came before. The primed-schema-key idea is moot: seon.db/prime-commit! was replaced by a Datahike pin (e2cc093281, stored->db seeds the schema key). seon.db/ids! runs before the :state mark, so it is not in the measured span; what is: begin-run! (begin-run-tx!, task/agent-cites-tx on the agent's first run) and the serve loop's set-up. Measured 2026-11-01 (40 agents in turn in one shared test root, each from its :state mark to its first request): 5.1 MB at the first, 13.2 MB at the 40th, ~0.2 MB more an earlier agent, with spikes (12.3 MB at the 16th, 21 MB at the 31st). Split at begin-run!'s end: begin-run! grows 2.5 -> ~9 MB (17 MB spikes), the rest 4.3 -> 5.4 MB. Within begin-run! (seon.profile, an epoch opened just before it), self bytes at agents 1 / 21 / 41: seon.ctx/own 0.57 / 2.14 / 4.33 MB in the same 12 calls, seon.agent/transact! 0.76 / 1.83 / 2.32 MB in 2, seon.ref/page-top 1.19 MB at 41 only; the rest flat. And seon.ctx/own's pulls answer the same entities at the same size at every agent (the largest a section of 3.9 KB, 17 references): so it is not more data read but the same d/pull of the same entity costing more as the root's history grows, as does a transaction. A warm pull in one process is flat (measured: d/pull [*] of one fixed entity on a test branch after 10, 30, 100, 300 small transactions: 26,656 bytes each time, datoms 5,976 -> 7,426), so the growth is in the forked agent's cold read of its branch (opening it at spawn, decoding nodes into an empty cache) or in what its branch holds at spawn. Nor is it the agent's cold read: in each of 40 agents in one root, d/pull [*] of its task entity costs 47,728 bytes the first time and 47,664 the second at every agent, while the branch grows 111 datoms an agent (174,177 -> 178,491). So seon.ctx/own's growth is in what it does beside a [*] pull of an existing db: it reads (dbi/-rschema db)'s :db.cardinality/many on a db value begin-run! just transacted, and pulls with an attribute pattern of its spec; seon.agent/transact! grows alongside, which points at work per transaction that grows with history (a schema index rebuilt on a new db value, a history index insert, a commit's diff). Measured apart, after start-up, in each of 40 agents: one transact! ~140 KB, (dbi/-rschema db) 560 bytes, seon.ctx/own of the task entity 49,232 bytes, its pull 47,664, flat at every agent. So nothing grows once the agent is running: the growth is only in its first moments after the fork, in begin-run!, before anything is warm. Two causes fit, to tell apart next: the agent catching up main's changes since the fork server's image (linear in a root's history when the root never restarts, as a shared test root does; seon.procs-test/the-fork-servers-replica-is-main-at-each-commit says the replica follows main, so measure what an agent's first read finds there), or decoding the index paths main's commits rewrote since that image. The first is ruled out by design: seon.agent/misaligned records a :seon.agent/fork-misaligned problem when an agent's image is not at main's head at spawn, so an agent never catches up main itself. Left: decoding the index nodes begin-run!'s first reads touch on the agent's freshly opened branch connection (its :store-reopened mark), whose count may grow with main's commits since the fork server last read them. Measure the node decodes (Datahike's store reads) of begin-run! at agents 1 and 40. Earlier idea, superseded: measure Datahike's read of one entity on a branch db as main's history grows, cold (datoms count, index depth, the unflushed tail a seek scans, node decodes in a fresh fork), in a test that writes n runs' worth of facts to main and pulls one entity on a branch cut after them, n in [10 30 100]: a cost proof that a pull of a fixed entity costs log n, not n. To measure it again: a scratch test calling seon.agent-test/in-agent 40 times in one root, reading (:marks @seon.agent/state); a mark after begin-run! in seon.agent's init, a seon.profile/begin! before it and (seon.profile/touched) after; and seon.ctx/own's pulled maps' sizes logged. Correct: an agent's first request allocates the same whatever ran before it (the primed key follows main's schema, or the hash costs what changed), and the test's number says so in any shard order."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-allocates-little-from-ready-to-its-first-request}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reader-of-an-agents-branch-sees-what-its-answered-evals-wrote"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch went red once in batch 66's merge prove (2026-10-31, at ff5f74f128; a 44-test shard of process tests) and green alone twice at the same tree: the agent answered its edit! (:edited), then root's eval read the agent's branch (seon.root/branch-db) and its :var/form for the edited var held the old source, \"[] 2\" missing. So an eval's answer can reach its asker before the branch commit that holds what the eval wrote is readable by another process (seon.repl's serve loop commits after the answer when :dirty?, agent/commit!). AGENTS section 5: a write is answered when committed in memory, and one followed by an outside effect waits for the sync that covers it; an outside reader acting on an answer is such an effect. Reproduce first: repeat the test's three steps in one shard with load beside it, and count. Correct: what an answered eval wrote is on the branch any reader opens after the answer (commit before answering, or the answer carries the commit and branch-db waits for it); no wait by clock."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch}}]
